---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick, mcpJson, site } from '../content/site';

const title = 'Cursor memory between sessions, with MCP | hippo';
const description =
  "Hippo gives Cursor's agent a local memory store over MCP, shared with Claude Code and Codex, with no account. Cursor gets an instruction block and the memory tools, and MCP access alone does not capture a session.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Cursor" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro>
      <Fragment slot="title">Cursor memory <span class="text-gradient">between sessions</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        What Cursor's agent worked out yesterday is gone today unless something wrote it down. Hippo is a local store that
        the agent reads and writes through MCP, shared with Claude Code, Codex and your other agents, with no account and
        no API key. Cursor gets an instruction block and the memory tools. That is lighter than the hooks Claude Code
        gets, and MCP access alone doesn't capture a session.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Set it up">
      <Cmd lines={[site.installCmd, 'hippo init']} copy />
      <p>
        <code>hippo init</code> adds its instructions to the project's <code>AGENTS.md</code> if it has one, and Cursor
        reads that file from the project root. Then add hippo to <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>.
      </p>
      <Cmd lines={mcpJson} prompt={false} copy />
      <p>
        With no global install, <code>"command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "hippo-memory", "mcp"]</code> works too. The
        <a class="link" href="/mcp/">MCP server page</a> lists all 13 tools.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Bring your rules with you" alt>
      <Cmd lines={['hippo import --cursor .cursor/rules --dry-run', 'hippo import --cursor .cursor/rules']} />
      <p>
        The import reads every <code>.mdc</code> and <code>.md</code> file under <code>.cursor/rules</code>, and an older
        single <code>.cursorrules</code> file, and turns them into memories your other agents can recall. Run the dry run
        first to see what it would add.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="One store for every agent">
      <p>
        Use Claude Code on Monday, Cursor on Tuesday and Codex on Wednesday, and they all read the same
        <code>.hippo/</code> store. Keep Cursor's rules for standing instructions. Hippo holds what comes up as the agent
        works, and lets you mark a lesson wrong with <code>hippo outcome --bad</code> or replace a fact with
        <code>hippo supersede</code>.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq
      items={faqPick(['How do I give Cursor memory between sessions?'])}
      heading="Cursor questions"
      moreHref="/faq/"
    />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
